  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSoutheasterly flow on the northeast flank of the remnants of\nIsaias strengthens Saturday afternoon and holds into midday\nSunday. Forecast profiles place a 40 to 65KT low-level jet in a\nnarrow stable layer near H850. Isentropic downglide off the \nhigher terrain is present but not especially strong, so most of \nthat momentum stays aloft. Where the stable layer is thin and \nthe terrain forces descent, a significant fraction can still mix\ndown at least in a spatially isolated fashion, generally \nconfined to where isentropes intersect terrain or any hydraulic \njumps. This supports gusts of 30 to 45 mph in the lee of the \nhigher terrain and on exposed ridgetops, with an extremely \nisolated gust near 60 mph possible. A wind advisory has been \nissued for these areas from Saturday afternoon through midday \nSunday primarily for low end advisory level gusts with isolated \ninstances of high end advisory or low end warning gusts.\n\nElsewhere, gusts of 20 to 30 mph are possible late Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning. Those winds should ease through \nSunday afternoon as the saturated layer deepens and the stronger\nflow aloft has a harder time reaching the ground. \n\nWith leaves still on the trees, at least some rain loading and a\nfairly long period without significant widespread gusts, some\nalready weakened trees will likely fall across the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nTropically influenced moisture begins to reach the area as \nearly as Saturday morning. Rain Saturday afternoon into \nSaturday evening should be light and spotty, favoring the \nmountains first and leaving some downslope areas dry at the \nstart. The heavier rates arrive early Sunday morning into Sunday\nafternoon, when modest but deep instability overlaps deep warm-\ncloud depths. The heaviest rain should sit on the eastern \nslopes of the higher terrain, where the southeasterly jet is \nupslope, and across parts of the Middle Ohio Valley, where \nbanded precipitation tied to the core of the Isaias circulation.\n\nMost locations can expect a half inch to two inches. Strong\ndownslope corridors, including parts of the Metro Valley early\nin the event, may stay on the low end. Persistent banding or a\nmore persistent upslope plume can exceed two inches locally. \nSoils are dry after several rain-free days, and this rain should\nbe primarily beneficial. Isolated high water is still possible \nwhere a heavier rate sits on a narrow hollow, a poor-drainage \nspot, or a storm drain clogged by blowing leaves. Overall,\nlimited issues, primarily nuisance type are possible, but\nwidespread or significant flooding is not expected.\n\nRain tapers Sunday night into Monday morning as the weakening\nsystem lifts toward the Atlantic. High pressure returns briefly\nMonday into Tuesday. Another weak cold front Tuesday night\nbrings a chance of light rain and a small cool down for\nWednesday, with no significant wind or flood concern attached to\nthat front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nSunday afternoon has a narrow window for surface-based storms on\nthe eastern flank of the low-level circulation. Strong\nsoutheasterly downslope should keep little if any rain falling\nin the Tri-State, the Metro Valley, and the southern coalfields\nfor a time, which allows some heating. With the circulation now\nnorth and east of its previous position, that dry slot lines up\nover those lowlands rather than farther west. If surface parcels\ncan warm sufficiently to ascent freely above the advertised\nstable layer, they would do so into enlarged low-level \nhodographs on the flank of the remnant circulation, an \nenvironment that can support rotating showers or a brief \ntornado. \n\nConfidence is low and it will not take much leftover cloud or a\nslight jog in the rain shield to kill the heating. This is a \nconditional, limited-area threat for Sunday afternoon. The more \nprobable outcome remains a wind-driven rain event with only \nisolated thunder.",
